.TH AZURE-PROTECTED-SECRETS-TOOL 1 "2026-05-04" "azure-protected-vm-secrets 1.0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
azure-protected-secrets-tool \- inspect Azure Confidential VM state and unprotect host-provisioned secrets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B azure-protected-secrets-tool
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENT ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B azure-protected-secrets-tool
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B azure-protected-secrets-tool
is the command-line front-end to the Azure CVM Secrets Provisioning Library
(SSPL). It detects the confidential-computing isolation environment of the
host and decrypts secrets that were provisioned to the VM by the Azure
control plane and sealed to the virtual TPM (vTPM).
.PP
The commands that touch the vTPM
.RB ( is-secrets-provisioning-enabled ", " unprotect-secret )
typically require access to the TPM resource-manager device
.RB ( /dev/tpmrm0 )
and therefore must be run as root on a Confidential VM. The
.B is-cvm
command only reads the hardware report and does not require elevated
privileges.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B is-cvm
Report the confidential-computing isolation type of the host. Output is of
the form
.IR "isolation_type=<TYPE>" " " "hypervisor=<NAME>" ,
where
.I TYPE
is
.BR SNP " (AMD SEV-SNP), " TDX " (Intel TDX), or " NONE
(not a Confidential VM). Returns a non-zero exit status when the host is
not a Confidential VM.
.TP
.B is-secrets-provisioning-enabled
Report whether secrets provisioning has been enabled on this VM by checking
for the presence of the provisioning key in the vTPM. Prints
.IR "enabled=true" " or " "enabled=false" .
.TP
.BI "unprotect-secret " [TOKEN]
Decrypt a host-protected secret. The protected secret is supplied as a
JSON Web Token (JWT), either as the inline
.I TOKEN
positional argument or, when omitted, read from standard input. The
decrypted secret is written to standard output.
.TP
.B validate-imds-metadata
Perform two-level verification of the Azure Instance Metadata Service
(IMDS) attestation blob.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-\-policy " N
For
.BR unprotect-secret ,
set the unseal policy bitmask to the unsigned integer
.IR N .
When omitted the policy defaults to 0.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit machine-readable JSON instead of the default human-readable output.
Supported by the reporting commands (for example
.BR is-cvm ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the library version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR is-cvm ,
indicates the host is a Confidential VM.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred, or for
.B is-cvm
the host is not a Confidential VM.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Detect the isolation type on a CVM:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ azure-protected-secrets-tool is-cvm
isolation_type=SNP hypervisor=Microsoft Hv
.EE
.RE
.PP
The same, as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ azure-protected-secrets-tool is-cvm --json
{"isolation_type":"SNP","hypervisor":"Microsoft Hv"}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Unprotect a secret supplied on standard input, applying policy 2:
.PP
.RS
.EX
$ azure-protected-secrets-tool unprotect-secret --policy 2 < token.jwt
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
Project homepage:
.UR https://github.com/Azure/confidential-computing-cvm-guest-attestation
.UE
